window.docContents[165].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.SignalSession.CliElf/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___SignalSession___CliElf":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession › CliElf.leanTier 1 · statement file\n\nTier 1 · statement file used by the symbolic proof 4 headline theorems depend on it 75 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Read in full. A statement file: its definitions say what the headline theorems claim (games, adversaries, bounds). Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.This file is in the non-default library CliElfLib (its theorems need the ELF built from source). Its theorems are not among the headline theorems that the closure tool lists.\n\n\n\nThe libsignal-cli ELF, built from sourceTrusted: the statement. cliElfBytes is the raw byte sequence of .GeneratedLean/cli-elf/libsignal-cli.elf, read at elaboration time via file_bytes% (Arm64/Trusted/Process.lean), which verifies the SHA-256 digest pinned below.Where the file comes from. It is not committed (.GeneratedLean/ is gitignored). scripts/cli-elf-stock --stdbin --build builds it from source with stock LLVM ld.lld (-static -no-pie --no-relax --gc-sections, linker script third_party/libsignal/libsignal-session/cli-elf/stock.ld, load base 0x400000), with the libsignal revision, rustc 1.98.1 (and its aarch64-unknown-linux-musl self-contained/ CRT objects and libc.a), zig (for rdl_shim.S), ld.lld version prefix, std-main entry crate, and arm64-semantics commit pinned in third_party/libsignal/libsignal-session/cli-elf/stock/inputs_stock_stdbin.lock, from the same Rust objects that rust-obj2lean reads: the compiled crate objects and trimmed alloc/std sysroot members of libsignal-session/translation.toml (cliLinkObjs, extracted into libsignalSessionLib = libsignalSessionMultiLib), and the libsignal-cli-io crate object of cli/io/translation.toml (SignalSessionIoPlacedObj). In CI, the parallel cli-elf-build job builds the ELF from source, checks its SHA-256 and link metadata against the lock and this module's pin (scripts/cli-elf-stock --stdbin --verify-pin --lean-mod LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/CliElf.lean), checks structural invariants and separate-path-depth deterministic reproducibility (scripts/cli-elf-stock --stdbin --verify and --check), and runs the A-LINK linker-resolution check (scripts/stock-pin/alink_check.py --lock third_party/libsignal/libsignal-session/cli-elf/stock/m4_stock.lock); the lake build job then downloads the verified ELF artifact, re-checks --verify-pin, and builds and audits the lake library CliElfLib (which owns this module and lists .GeneratedLean/cli-elf/libsignal-cli.elf as an input_file; no default lake root imports this module). These jobs and steps run unconditionally on every push to main (and workflow_dispatch) and on pull_requests touching the ELF's byte or build inputs, trusted specifications, or the CliElfLib-only proof modules (scripts/clielf_ci_paths.txt); a PR that modifies only shared upstream proof modules skips them, so any breakage it introduces in the CliElfLib-only modules surfaces on the first main push that contains it, and the binary-level verification claim refers to a main commit SHA whose cli-elf-build and lake build jobs ran the CLI ELF steps green.What the pin means. Anyone can rebuild the ELF from source and compare its SHA-256 with the pin. The pin is the only place where a byte of the ELF is named. Every address the statement uses comes from the ELF itself (ElfImage.parse, loadState) or from libsignalSessionLib and VerifiedLibraries.mallocngLib under kernel-checked MultiPlacements mpl and mmpl (CliBinaryPq.lean, stock ld.lld link under stock.ld at base 0x400000; name labels: CliLabelsFromObjects (CliLabels.lean)).Main definitions* cliElfBytes: the raw bytes of the pinned libsignal-cli ELF, the subject of every binary-level CLI statement (CliBinaryPq.lean, CliBinaryExtProofDefs.lean).\n\n\n\ndef cliElfBytesL68–73 ↗#Raw bytes of the static ARM64 libsignal-cli ELF (ET_EXEC, musl), verified against its pinned digest by file_bytes%.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/CliElf.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.SignalSession.CliElf/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___SignalSession___CliElf"}});